//! SOS kernel core (designs 112, 140, 162) — the arch-free kernel.
//!
//! Everything a SOS kernel image shares: the console, the trap handler `ktrap`,
//! the object-op dispatch, and the sosimg loader. An image is one per-arch
//! kernel HAL (`sos/hal/<arch>/kernel/`) + the shared runtime seams
//! (`sosrt`, sos/rt/common/src/lib.saw) + this module + one entry `.saw` defining
//! `kmain` — `sos/kernel/main.saw` for the real kernel, `sos/tests/*.saw` for
//! the harness cases. Sharing the module is what keeps every test image on the
//! SAME trap path instead of a copy of it.
//!
//! NO ARCHITECTURE APPEARS IN THIS DIRECTORY (design 162 unit 1). Registers,
//! trap causes, protection hardware, the board's console and the way the machine
//! stops are all reached through `hal`, and the harness enforces it: a scan
//! rejects an arch name anywhere under `core/`, so the seam cannot erode by one
//! convenient constant at a time. Adding an architecture is writing a HAL.
//!
//! Addresses and machine words are `UInt` throughout, never `Int` — `Int` is
//! platform-width and SIGNED, so a RAM address lands in its negative half on a
//! 32-bit profile and means a different number on a 64-bit one (DF-140a).
//!
//! # THIS FILE IS THE FACADE (the Aug-17 split)
//!
//! The kernel used to be one 4000-line file. It is now thirteen, one per seam,
//! and this file holds no code: it re-exports the names an image entry uses, so
//! `import kcore.{console, load_root_and_enter}` in `main.saw` and in every
//! `sos/tests/` entry means exactly what it always did.
//!
//! THE MODULES, IN DEPENDENCY ORDER — a file may import only those above it,
//! because an import cycle is not diagnosed (DF-232e); it silently empties a
//! module's export table and blames a third file:
//!
//!   `limits`     every slab size, the frame stride, the "no slot" sentinels
//!   `mem`        addresses and byte moves
//!   `diag`       the console, and the reports that stop the machine
//!   `result`     a syscall's answer, and the one place it enters a frame
//!   `objects`    the handle table and the validated per-kind objects
//!   `threads`    the thread slab, the frame arena, the ready queue
//!   `time`       the Clock/Timer slabs and the one hardware comparator
//!   `waitables`  the Event/Waiter/Interrupt slabs and the per-kind matrix
//!   `process`    the copy doors, the teardown, the way into user mode
//!   `wake`       delivery: readiness becoming a woken thread
//!   `irq`        the tick, expiry, device lines, the idle path
//!   `sched`      the switch point
//!   `dispatch`   every object op, and `ktrap`
//!   `loader`     the sosimg loader
//!
//! WHERE THE LINE FELL, when a seam of the sketch came in two: the STATE of a
//! thing sits below the process teardown that frees it, and ACTING on it sits
//! above — because ending a process touches every slab, and answering a wait
//! goes through the copy door that can end one. That is why a thread's table
//! (`threads`) is not its scheduler (`sched`), a timer's schedule (`time`) is
//! not its expiry (`irq`), and the wait tables (`waitables`) are not the
//! delivery (`wake`).
//!
//! WHAT A SPLIT COSTS HERE (DF-232f): Saw has no package-internal visibility, so
//! every name two seams share is `public` — visible to anything that imports
//! `kcore`, not merely to its siblings. Design 80's gate no longer protects the
//! kernel's own tables from the kernel's own consumers. Nothing about the IMAGE
//! changed; the widening is a compile-time fact, and it is the argument for an
//! `internal` tier rather than a reason to keep one 4000-line file.

public import kcore.mem.{align_up}
public import kcore.diag.{Console, ExitCode, console, fatal, fatal_image}
public import kcore.time.{start_tick}
public import kcore.process.{start_process}
public import kcore.irq.{timer_ticks}
public import kcore.dispatch.{ktrap}
public import kcore.loader.{load_root_and_enter}
